  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 319 PM EST TUE NOV 10 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, midlevel troughing will be \nlocated over the eastern CONUS, with amplified midlevel ridging \nover the Intermountain West and High Plains. Farther west, two \nclosed lows will be located off the Pacific Coast: one near \nBritish Columbia, and another off the coast of California. Closer \nto eastern Kentucky, multiple shortwaves will be embedded within \nthe eastern CONUS trough, with the first shortwave on the eastern \nfringes of the forecast area on Thursday morning. At the surface, \nan area of low pressure associated with this feature will be \nlocated in the Mid Atlantic region, with a secondary cold front \ninitially just to our north. This front will progress through \neastern Kentucky during the day on Thursday, with winds becoming \nmore northwesterly as the front passes. Associated CAA will keep \ntemperatures below average throughout the day. Thus, expect a non-\ndiurnal temperature curve during the day on Thursday, with highs \noccurring in the morning for most locations (generally in the low \n50s), although with the caveat that some frontal timing \ndiscrepancies still exist within deterministic guidance. \n\nMore interestingly, this system could bring the first snowflakes \nof the season to eastern Kentucky. Rain will be likely during the \nday on Thursday as temperatures remain fairly warm (in the 40s and\n50s for all but the highest elevations). Multiple shortwaves \nshould provide ample lift for precipitation on Thursday and \nFriday, and upslope flow via northwesterly post-frontal winds \nshould also enhance these chances, particularly given the \ndeterministic model signal for this flow to interact with a \nmoisture plume originating from Lake Michigan. Given this \nfavorable setup, PoPs were manually increased from the NBM \nbaseline, with the greatest PoPs in the high terrain of \nsoutheastern Kentucky. By Friday morning, cooler air aloft could \nsupport the transition to a mixture of rain and snow for much of \nthe forecast area, although with relatively warm surface \ntemperatures, rain should still be the primary precipitation type \nin most locations, except in the highest terrain. Some winter \nprecipitation could linger in far eastern Kentucky on Saturday, \nalthough PoPs should decline beyond this point as heights rise and\na drier pattern sets in.\n\nA calmer pattern will return by Sunday, with tranquil weather \nexpected through the remainder of the period. Aloft, the ridge \ninitially over the Intermountain West will shift east as the \naforementioned Pacific systems move onshore, with the original \ndeep trough now east of the forecast area. Thus, at the surface, \nhigh pressure will set in, with dry and uneventful weather \nexpected. High temperatures will trend warmer under the influence \nof aforementioned height rises, though winds will still maintain a\nlight northerly component around the surface high, keeping maxTs \nnear to slightly below average for this time of year despite ample\nsunshine. Overnight, lows will be cool, falling into the 20s in \nmany valley locations.",
